<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teatro San Cassiano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teatro San Cassiano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was the first public opera house in the world, opened in 1637 by the Tron family, where audiences paid for admission instead of attending by invitation at court. Its success led to many more commercial opera houses in Venice, which became the leading centre of opera in the 17th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was demolished in 1812.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-san-cassiano.jpg" alt="Teatro San Cassiano" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Secchi Smith, ©Teatro San Cassiano Ltd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_San_Cassiano_reimagined.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
